# CV project agent standards

These rules are mandatory for every proposal, edit, commit, and push in this repo.

## 0. DESIGN CLARITY ABOVE ALL (most important)

**Clarity beats everything else** — aesthetics, cleverness, density, decoration.

- Cleanness of the site is the #1 product goal
- Least amount of text that still communicates; no chaos, no clutter, no competing focal points
- One clear hierarchy per view; whitespace over boxes; restraint over ornament
- A person using a screen reader (or otherwise reading without sighted layout cues) must be able to understand **all** content without friction
- Prefer semantic HTML (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `h1`–`h3`, lists, `button`/`a` correctly), meaningful link text, labels, and logical reading order
- Strong contrast, readable type size, clear focus states — accessibility is part of clarity, not an afterthought
- If a design choice adds noise without aiding understanding, remove it

Cross-examine every change:

> Is this the clearest possible version?

If not, simplify before shipping.

## 1. AAA modular design = general software modularity

Before creating, changing, proposing, or pushing anything, cross-examine it:

> Is this following modular, production-quality software design?

Meaning for this repo:

- **Shared CSS is mandatory** — one design system / stylesheet modules reused across pages
- **Never** invent different one-off styles per subpage that drift from the system
- Structure / presentation / behavior stay separated and reusable
- Predictable file layout; easy to add a page or portfolio item without rewriting the base
- No copy-paste CSS/HTML duplication when a shared class or partial pattern would do
- Clean code above clever code

Mirror what people treat as “perfect clean design”: clarity, hierarchy, restraint, consistency, accessibility — not visual noise.

## 2. PC + mobile parity (PC primary)

- **Primary target:** desktop / PC layout and polish
- **Required:** iPad and mobile must show the **same high visual quality**, not a degraded fallback
- Design both from the start — never “fix later”
- Touch targets, type, spacing, nav, and controls must feel intentional on iPad
- Avoid hover-only UX, tiny hit areas, overflow, or desktop-only assumptions

## 3. Ask questions aggressively

- Whenever even **slightly** unsure — **ask**
- No limit on how many questions; more is better than guessing
- Prefer short, concrete questions (options when helpful)
- Do not invent portfolio facts, bio, jobs, or major visual direction when uncertain
- **Portfolio placeholders stay until the user replaces them** — do not delete or rewrite with fake “real” entries unless asked

## 4. Extensive visual QA before every push (hard gate)

UI changes require **super extensive** testing. Agent viewport testing is enough (user is not required to re-check on device), but the bar is high.

**Do not push UI until all of the below pass** at minimum:

### Viewports (test all)
- Desktop wide (~1440px)
- Desktop medium (~1280px)
- iPad landscape (~1024–1180px)
- iPad portrait (~768–834px)
- Mobile (~390px)

### Surfaces to verify on each viewport
- Landing page and every subpage touched
- Top nav / Portfolio button / home link — must look like intentional controls, **never** default blue underlined browser links
- Typography hierarchy, spacing rhythm, overflow, wrapping
- Expand/collapse (portfolio details): open + closed states
- Focus/keyboard affordances where relevant
- Contrast and readability of brand, body, muted text, accents

### Method
- Use browser + screenshots (or equivalent) in this environment
- Compare PC vs iPad/mobile captures side by side mentally against clarity + parity rules
- Call out any residual risk before push; if blocked, ask — do not push blind

**Push rule:** no UI push without completed extensive visual QA evidence (screenshots / recorded checks).
